    "debrief":  {
                    "realTeam":  "In 2013 the Curiosity team saw holes and tears in the rover\u0027s thin aluminum wheels, made by sharp rocks. They started planning routes over smoother ground and checked the wheels with Curiosity\u0027s own camera. In 2017 they sent new software that changes how fast each wheel spins, so the wheels push less hard on sharp rocks. Curiosity kept driving for many more years.",
                    "tryNext":  "Pick smooth routes, fix the rover on calm days, and keep the Science lab on to study your rocks for extra research.",
                    "realScience":  {
                                        "title":  "Why rover wheels are thin",
                                        "text":  "Every kilogram sent to Mars costs a lot of fuel, so rover parts are made as light as possible. Thin wheels are light, but sharp rocks can poke holes in them.",
